Phantom-WG Modern — Migration Guide
=====================================

From 1.1.5 to 2.0.0. This file is the draft of the migration document: the
steps, and under "What Was Measured" the facts they rest on.

Before You Start:
  2.0.0 is installed next to 1.1.5, in a directory of its own. The data and
  the environment files of 1.1.5 are copied into it; the directory of 1.1.5
  stays as it is and is the way back (see Rollback).

  What carries over as it is:
    - The WireGuard server keys: the client configs of 1.1.5 keep working
    - Clients, the address pool, multihop exits and the firewall state
    - Users, their roles and passwords, TOTP, and the audit log
    - The TLS certificate, self-signed or from Let's Encrypt
    - .env.daemon

  The stack is down from the down of 1.1.5 (step 3) to the up of 2.0.0
  (step 7).

  The examples run as root, as the 1.1.5 quick start does, with 1.1.5 in
  /opt/phantom-wg and 2.0.0 in /opt/phantom-wg-modern. Any other directory
  works the same way. A git clone without a directory name takes
  phantom-wg, which in /opt is the directory of 1.1.5: git stops there.

Host Requirements:
  2.0.0 asks more of the host than 1.1.5:

    1.1.5   Docker Engine 20.10+, Docker Compose v2, bash, git
    2.0.0   Docker Engine 27.0.1+, Docker Compose 2.27.2+, kernel 5.15+,
            /dev/net/tun, python3 3.9+, bash, git

  ./tools/prod.sh gates of 2.0.0 reports what the host has (step 2). The
  tools stop on a python3 under 3.9; setup and up stop on a kernel under
  5.15, without /dev/net/tun, or when Docker or Compose does not answer. The
  versions of Docker and Compose are reported, not compared: check them
  against the list above.

What Changes:
  .env (new)
    PHANTOM_IMAGE_SOURCE     registry: the images come from the registry,
                             pinned by the release. 1.1.5 built the daemon
                             and auth-service images on the host and ran
                             nginx:1-alpine.
    PHANTOM_SUBNET_V4        The network of the stack. 1.1.5 fixed it in
    PHANTOM_SUBNET_V6        docker-compose.yml; the defaults are the same,
                             172.28.0.0/24 and fd00:d0ce::/64.

  docker-compose.override.yml (new)
    The published ports, 51820/udp and 443 as in 1.1.5. 1.1.5 kept them in
    docker-compose.yml.

  .env.daemon
    The same keys and the same defaults: the file of 1.1.5 is used as it is.

  .env.auth-service
    AUTH_PROXY_MAX_BODY         67108864 in 1.1.5, 65536 in 2.0.0: the
                                largest request body through the API
    AUTH_PROXY_BACKUP_MAX_BODY  New, 67108864: the largest backup upload
    AUTH_TRUSTED_PROXIES        In 1.1.5 it replaced the networks
                                auth-service trusts; in 2.0.0 it adds to the
                                subnets of the stack

    The file of 1.1.5 works as it is: without AUTH_PROXY_BACKUP_MAX_BODY,
    2.0.0 takes 67108864. With AUTH_PROXY_MAX_BODY=67108864 every API
    request may carry 64 MiB, where 2.0.0 sets 64 KiB.

  tools/
    ./tools/prod.sh stays the entry; the commands run on python3. New:
    gates, images, pull, add-superadmin, cloudflare. Gone: compose, use
    docker compose in the directory instead. What the tools write belongs to
    the owner of the install directory.

Migration:
  1. Look at 1.1.5

       cd /opt/phantom-wg
       ./tools/prod.sh status
       git status --short

     git status lists the files changed on this host. A changed
     docker-compose.yml holds ports or subnets of its own: note them for
     step 6.

  2. Get 2.0.0 next to it, and check the host

       git clone https://github.com/ARAS-Workspace/phantom-wg.git \
         /opt/phantom-wg-modern

     or from a release zip:

       cd /opt
       unzip phantom-wg-modern-v2.0.0-amd64.zip    # or -arm64

     then:

       cd /opt/phantom-wg-modern
       ./tools/prod.sh gates

  3. Stop 1.1.5

       cd /opt/phantom-wg
       ./tools/prod.sh down

     The databases are copied in step 4: a running stack could leave them
     halfway written.

  4. Copy the data and the environment files

       cd /opt/phantom-wg-modern
       cp -a /opt/phantom-wg/container-data /opt/phantom-wg/.env.daemon \
         /opt/phantom-wg/.env.auth-service .
       chown -R --reference=. container-data .env.daemon .env.auth-service

     cp -a keeps the owners of 1.1.5. chown gives the copy to the owner of
     this directory, the user the tools of 2.0.0 write as; as root in a
     directory of root it changes nothing.

  5. Write what 2.0.0 adds

       ./tools/prod.sh setup

     setup creates only what is missing: here .env and
     docker-compose.override.yml, each from its .example. The keys, the auth
     database, the TLS certificate and the address pool of 1.1.5 are in
     place, and setup leaves them.

  6. Organize the environment files

     .env.auth-service, as 2.0.0 sets it:
       AUTH_PROXY_MAX_BODY=65536
       AUTH_PROXY_BACKUP_MAX_BODY=67108864

     Ports of its own from step 1: docker-compose.override.yml.
     Subnets of its own from step 1: PHANTOM_SUBNET_V4 and PHANTOM_SUBNET_V6
     in .env.
     A WIREGUARD_LISTEN_PORT of its own in .env.daemon: the same port as the
     container side of the udp entry in docker-compose.override.yml.

  7. Start 2.0.0

       ./tools/prod.sh up
       ./tools/prod.sh status
       ./tools/prod.sh show-versions

Check:
  - The dashboard answers, and the admin of 1.1.5 logs in with the same
    password
  - The clients of 1.1.5 are listed
  - A client connects with its config of 1.1.5: its handshake shows in the
    dashboard
  - The audit log lists the entries of 1.1.5 before the new ones

Rollback:
  The directory of 1.1.5 is as it was: step 4 copied from it.

    cd /opt/phantom-wg-modern
    ./tools/prod.sh down
    cd /opt/phantom-wg
    ./tools/prod.sh up

  What 2.0.0 wrote after step 7 (new clients, users, audit entries) stays in
  /opt/phantom-wg-modern; 1.1.5 does not have it.

After Migration:
  Let's Encrypt: its state is in container-data/certbot, copied in step 4. A
  cron job or timer that renews the certificate names the directory of
  1.1.5: point it to the new one.

  Images of 1.1.5: they were built on this host, under the name of its
  directory. To list and remove them:

    docker image ls
    docker image rm <the images of 1.1.5>

  The directory of 1.1.5 is the way back: remove it once 2.0.0 serves.

  Updates of 2.0.0: see UPDATE.

What Was Measured:
  1.1.5 is main at v1.1.5 (8de545c3), 2.0.0 is dev/daemon before its
  release. Read from the two trees:

  Install
    - The 1.1.5 quick start installs as root: get.phantom.tc makes a
      shallow, sparse git clone of main in /opt/phantom-wg.
    - The 2.0.0 release text clones into phantom-wg and unzips into
      phantom-wg-modern.

  Data
    - Both docker-compose.yml bind the same paths: container-data/db,
      container-data/state/db, container-data/auth-db and the six files of
      container-data/secrets/production. The containers have the same
      names: phantom-daemon, phantom-auth, phantom-nginx.
    - The tools of 1.1.5 make secrets/production and auth-db as the user
      who runs them; Docker makes db and state/db as root on the first up.
      2.0.0 makes db as the owner of the install directory, and Docker
      makes state/db as root, as in 1.1.5.
    - auth-service is 1.1.7 in 1.1.5 and 1.3.0 in 2.0.0. Its schema.sql
      differs in one comment; the tables of tools/bootstrap.py are the same.
      Both bootstraps make the admin a superadmin.
    - The repositories and the firewall state of the daemon keep their
      schemas; db.py migrates what it finds.

  Environment
    - .env.daemon.example: the same keys and the same values.
    - .env.auth-service.example: AUTH_PROXY_MAX_BODY 67108864 to 65536,
      AUTH_PROXY_BACKUP_MAX_BODY added with 67108864, the default of the
      code when the key is missing.
    - AUTH_TRUSTED_PROXIES: 1.1.5 reads it in place of its default
      networks, 2.0.0 adds it to PHANTOM_SUBNET_V4 and PHANTOM_SUBNET_V6.
    - .env and docker-compose.override.yml.example: in 2.0.0 only.

  Tools
    - 2.0.0 setup refuses a complete install and otherwise creates only
      what is missing: it writes .env and docker-compose.override.yml from
      their examples, and leaves keys, auth database, TLS certificate and
      address pool it finds.
    - 1.1.5 renews no certificate by itself; 2.0.0 leaves the schedule to
      the operator. Both keep the state in container-data/certbot.

  Still to measure, in the rehearsal:
    - setup of step 5 on the data of a real 1.1.5 (read from the code, not
      run)
    - The names of the 1.1.5 images to remove
    - The entries of 1.1.5 in the audit log of the 2.0.0 dashboard
    - A client of 1.1.5 connecting to 2.0.0
    - Pulling the images of 2.0.0 before step 3, to shorten the down time
